    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "methode",
     "title": "Relative and absolute references",
     "md": "When a formula is copied, a **relative reference** (B2) changes with the new position. An **absolute reference** (\\$B\\$2) stays fixed. Use it for a single value used by many rows, such as a tax rate in cell \\$E\\$1."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "- Typing the result instead of a formula: it will not update.\n- Forgetting the = sign: the cell shows text.\n- Copying a formula that needs \\$ and getting wrong answers.\n- Choosing a pie chart for data that is not parts of a whole."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 1 - Formulas",
     "statement": "Rice: 3 bags at 600 FCFA each in B2 and C2. Write the formula for the total in D2, and a formula to total D2:D3.",
     "steps": [
      "D2 = B2\\*C2 = 3 x 600 = 1800.",
      "If D3 is 3000, the grand total is =SUM(D2:D3) = 4800."
     ],
     "answer": "=B2\\*C2 ; =SUM(D2:D3)"
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 2 - Absolute reference",
     "statement": "Prices are in C2:C4 and a discount rate of 10% is in E1. Write the formula for the discounted price in D2 that can be copied down.",
     "steps": [
      "Use the price C2 and the rate E1: =C2\\*(1-\\$E\\$1).",
      "\\$E\\$1 is absolute so it stays on E1 when copied to D3 and D4; C2 becomes C3, C4."
     ],
     "answer": "=C2\\*(1-\\$E\\$1)"
    }
   ],
